From: Pad 2 bring-up <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: retain OTG control after unplug

A captured PC-to-keyboard transition enables OTG AP control after USB goes
offline, then disables it 14 ms later on a second offline notification.
The first update incorrectly marks OTG active, so the second falls into the
catch-all disable branch. No host notification or keyboard enumeration follows.

Disable AP control only while USB power is online, track the online state
after every successful update, and leave OTG host state to the OTG
enable/disable notifications. Repeated offline notifications then preserve
the restored DRP/AP setup.

--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -1510,15 +1510,14 @@
 						    OPLUS_USB_TYPEC_MODE, 0);
 		if (ret)
 			goto out;
-		battmgr->oplus_otg_state = true;
-	} else {
+	} else if (battmgr->usb.online) {
 		ret = qcom_battmgr_request_property(battmgr, BATTMGR_USB_PROPERTY_SET,
 						    OPLUS_USB_OTG_AP_ENABLE, 0);
 		if (ret)
 			goto out;
-		battmgr->oplus_was_online = battmgr->usb.online;
-		battmgr->oplus_otg_state = false;
-	}
+	}
+	/* OTG notifications own oplus_otg_state; AP control is not host state. */
+	battmgr->oplus_was_online = battmgr->usb.online;
 out:
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err(battmgr->dev, "OnePlus USB state update failed: %d\n", ret);
